<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">
  <title>Entering Early and Emerging Adulthood</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00063</md:content-id>
    <md:uuid>36cac3c8-7fca-4b48-937e-b9bf854bca1e</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="learning-objectives" id="sect-00001">
    <title>Learning Objectives</title>
    <para id="para-00001">By the end of this section, you will be able to:</para>
    <list id="list-00001">
    <item>Identify the biological and cognitive markers of early and emerging adulthood</item>
    <item>Explain the major developmental tasks and expected outcomes of early/emerging adulthood</item>
    <item>Describe environmental factors that impact reaching physical and cognitive milestones in early/emerging adulthood</item>
    </list>
    </section>
    <para id="para-00002">Jabril graduated college last spring with a bachelor’s degree in biology and works in the pharmaceutical industry. It’s not exactly his dream job, but he knows the skills he develops will open different paths for the future. He moved back in with his parents to save money while working. This wasn’t his first choice either, but he knows that paying down his college debt will help him later. Besides, it allows him to reconnect with his younger sister, who is about to start high school. Jabril jogs in his neighborhood and has connected with a new temple after several years of questioning his religious beliefs. He isn’t currently in a relationship, but he peruses dating apps occasionally. The possibilities for his personal and professional life are quite open.</para>
    <para id="para-00003">Jabril is in a period of life traditionally known as early adulthood or <term id="term-00001">emerging adulthood</term>, a time of life between ages 18 and 25 and characterized by the achievement of new legal statuses (such as being able to vote, drive, drink alcohol, and marry) as well as intense exploration of career options, beliefs, and relationships. Until the early 1980s, people often started careers and got married—actions associated with full adulthood—right out of high school. But today, many countries around the world recognize a “buffer” period before full adulthood – a time when young adults may study in college or a vocational training program, explore career options through work, or travel. During this time, people also learn about the financial, personal, and social tasks of being an adult, such as making personal health decisions or living within a budget. They also become more accountable for the consequences of their choices, such as being responsible for debt they incur, having stiffer penalties for breaking the law, and being sued for actions that cause harm to others.</para>
    <para id="para-00004">In this section, we’ll examine theories of emerging adulthood, aspects of the physical and cognitive transitions to adulthood, common developmental tasks in emerging adulthood, and variations in the way people experience emerging adulthood across cultures.</para>
    <note class="link-to-learning" id="note-00001">
    <para id="para-00005">In this <link url="https://openstax.org/r/104TEDJay">TED talk, clinician Meg Jay</link> discusses emerging adulthood and what one can do to help plan for the future.</para>
    </note>
    <section id="sect-00002">
    <title>Theories of Early and /Emerging Adulthood</title>
    <para id="para-00006">Becoming an adult often involves making independent decisions and becoming more responsible for ourselves and others. As psychologists have researched this developmental stage, they have developed theories that can help to provide a framework and contextualization of the changes an individual undergoes during this phase of life.</para>
    <section id="sect-00003">
    <title>Five Challenges</title>
    <para id="para-00007">According to Jeffrey Arnett (2010), people face five challenges during emerging adulthood. Some of these challenges may first arise in adolescence, but Arnett believes we really work toward resolving them in emerging adulthood (Arnett et al., 2014) (<link target-id="fig-00001"/>).</para>
    <figure id="fig-00001">
    <media alt="Alt text placeholder">
    <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/OSX_Lifespan_11_01_Arnett.jpg"/>
    </media>
    <caption>Arnett’s theory of emerging adulthood proposes five challenges that needs to be resolved during this time in our lives. (attribution: Copyright Rice University, OpenStax, under CC BY 4.0 license)</caption>
    </figure>
    <para id="para-00008">Let’s examine each of these.</para>
    <list id="list-00002">
    <item><emphasis effect="italics">Identity exploration</emphasis>. Although this likely started in adolescence, people think more seriously about it in emerging adulthood (Arnett et al., 2014). They attempt to learn who they are as a person within multiple frameworks, such relationships and jobs, in addition to solidifying their core beliefs and values.</item>
    <item><emphasis effect="italics">Instability</emphasis>. Emerging adulthood is full of change, partly due to identity exploration, but also caused by factors such as being “last hired, first fired” at a job.</item>
    <item><emphasis effect="italics">Self-focus</emphasis>. Emerging adults may have fewer obligations to others than adolescents other age groups. This means they may be better able to focus on themselves to explore not just who they are, but how to become self-sufficient.</item>
    <item><emphasis effect="italics">Feeling in-between</emphasis>. Though legally they’re considered adults, emerging adults don’t always feel they are completely adults yet. They might not yet be comfortable making some decisions on their own and thus consult with family and friends when faced with important choices.</item>
    <item><emphasis effect="italics">Being in an age of possibilities</emphasis>. During this time of exploration, people can create their own stories, which may include leaving behind aspects of their early lives they felt were uncomfortable or unhealthy. Thus, despite some uncertainty and instability, emerging adults often report feeling optimistic about what lies ahead (Arnett et al., 2014).</item>
    </list>
    <note class="link-to-learning" id="note-00002">
    <para id="para-00009">The area of development occurring between adolescence and adulthood is relatively newer to be explored by lifespan psychologists, and has become popularized by the work of Jeffrey Arnett. Watch <link url="https://openstax.org/r/104TEDArnett">Arnett’s TED talk</link> in which he elaborates on work in this area and offers advice to emerging adults.</para>
    </note>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00004">
    <title>Four Identity Statuses</title>
    <para id="para-00010">James Marcia believed people experienced different stages in their quest for identity, based on their level of exploration and commitment (Erikson et al., 2020; Marcia, 1966; Kolbert et al., 2021). These stages are identity diffusion, identity foreclosure, identity moratorium, and identity achievement. Though these are presented as stages a person’s likely to progress through, anyone can move through them in various orders or even skip some of them. Also, because identity involves many aspects of ourselves, it’s possible for a person to be in different stages for different aspects of their identity. In other words, we don’t have to have everything figured out all at once.</para>
    <para id="para-00011">What does Marcia mean by identity diffusion, foreclosure, moratorium, and achievement?</para>
    <list id="list-00003">
    <item><emphasis effect="italics">Identity Diffusion</emphasis>. The individual has not yet explored options or has yet to realize they have choices; no exploration or commitment has occurred. Someone in identity diffusion may be aware of upcoming elections, but hasn’t thought about their political views or which candidates best represent them, and may not have even decided whether to vote.</item>
    <item><emphasis effect="italics">Identity Foreclosure</emphasis>. The individual has committed to some goals, values, or roles but without any exploration. They tend to conform to choices they see around them, those of their peers, or those espoused by their parents. A person who enters into an arranged marriage without actively considering whether the partner their parents have chosen is a good match for them has this status.</item>
    <item><emphasis effect="italics">Identity Moratorium</emphasis>. The individual is actively exploring options and may be ready to commit to some values, but has not yet fully committed to their choices. A college student who changes their major several times but never sticks with a specific one for very long may be experiencing identity moratorium.</item>
    <item><emphasis effect="italics">Identity Achievement</emphasis>. The individual has actively explored possibilities and has now made a commitment to them, meaning they have chosen an identity. A person who has read about different religious faiths and chosen a specific one to follow has attained identity achievement.</item>
    </list>
    <para id="para-00012">In general, identity achievement is associated with various aspects of psychosocial well-being, including high self-esteem, conscientiousness, maturity, and problem-focused coping (Oleś, 2016). However, some of the other stages may be appropriate under certain circumstances. For example, identity diffusion may be a natural starting point for people who have little experience (Marcia, 1966; Waterman, 1982). Also, as we’ll see in the next section, cultural and contextual factors may affect the search for identity, meaning that identity achievement may not be feasible or ideal for everyone.</para>
    <note class="life-hacks" id="note-00003">
    <title>Applying Marcia’s Theory to Your Life</title>
    <para id="para-00013">Establishing an identity, according to Marcia, requires both exploration and commitment. But it may not be obvious how to start exploring. Here are some ideas for how to do that.</para>
    <para id="para-00014">One thing you can do is consider your belief system, including not just formal views like religious and political beliefs but also what matters to you. Are you an animal lover? Do you think it’s important to reduce your carbon footprint? When it comes to money, are you a spender or a saver? What kind of medical care do you want (or not want) at the end of life? You can also think of specific characteristics, such as kindness, versatility, and advocacy, and the degree to which you value them in others and/or want to have them yourself. There aren’t any right or wrong answers here; these are all good qualities to have, but no one will have them all or find them all equally important. Asking yourself these questions or considering the personal qualities that matter to you can help you determine your belief system.</para>
    <para id="para-00015">You can also consider your life goals. Again, this involves not just formal goals like career aspirations and family plans, but also things you hope to achieve in life. What would a “perfect day” look like to you? Would you like to learn sign language or visit your ancestors’ homeland? How do you want to be remembered after you die? If you wrote a letter to your future self, what would you say?</para>
    <para id="para-00016">Now think about where you are on Marcia’s dimensions of exploration and commitment. You may not have previously thought about some of the issues presented, or perhaps you had considered them but not come to any real conclusions. Maybe now that you’ve learned about identity development and have seen some ideas for things you can explore, you can start moving closer to identity achievement.</para>
    </note>
    <note class="link-to-learning" id="note-00004">
    <para id="para-00017">The <link url="https://openstax.org/r/104ReflectTool">University of Edinburgh Reflectors’ Toolkit</link> contains several resources for identifying your goals and core values and connecting them to life experiences and career prospects.</para>
    </note>
    </section>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00005">
    <title>Contextual and Cultural Variations in the Experience of Emerging Adulthood</title>
    <para id="para-00018">Comparing the experience of emerging adulthood across countries contains a confound: Some factors, such as culture and SES, often occur together. We also need to acknowledge that increased globalization makes simple cultural distinctions (such as individualistic vs. collectivistic) difficult and perhaps outdated, as some traditionally collectivistic nations adopt more individualistic attitudes (Drobot, 2021; Sugimura, 2020). Thus, some transitions associated with early adulthood may not apply to all cultures, and the experiences of people in this age range may vary significantly.</para>
    <section id="sect-00006">
    <title>Socioeconomic Status: Impact on Timing of Early Adulthood Events</title>
    <para id="para-00019">In terms of Arnett’s theory, it appears that people experience emerging adulthood regardless of SES, but there are SES-related differences in how it’s experienced (Arnett, 2016; Galambos &amp; Martinez, 2007; Landberg et al., 2019; Wood et al., 2017). For example, the economic instability in many European countries means that young adults are likely to work on short-term contracts or in positions that could be terminated at any time (Arnett et al., 2014; Landberg et al., 2019), consistent with Arnett’s concept of instability. People from lower SES are more likely to have a negative outlook on life, and therefore be less likely to feel optimistic and see their life as full of possibilities. They may also lack opportunities for identity exploration due to life events such as being their family’s sole wage earner (Landberg et al., 2019). Applying this idea to Marcia’s theory, economic conditions may limit opportunities for both exploration and commitment (Beckert et al., 2020) or cause a person to prioritize gaining financial stability over traditional markers of identity achievement that typically occur in early adulthood stages of development (Bowen et al., 2021). People who can’t afford higher education may be limited to taking whatever job will pay the bills and not explore potentially more personally fulfilling careers (Haider &amp; von Stumm, 2022; Landberg et al., 2019; Wood et al., 2017) (<link target-id="fig-00002"/>). Coming from a lower SES doesn’t prevent a person from becoming an adult, but it tends to make the transition more difficult (National Research Council, 2005).</para>
    <figure id="fig-00002">
    <media alt="Alt text placeholder">
    <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/OSX_Lifespan_11_01_LowSES.jpg"/>
    </media>
    <caption>While any job has the potential to be fulfilling, people of low SES may be forced to take any job they can, regardless of how well it fulfills their life goals. (credit: modification of work “07 McDonald's fast food restaurant in Manila, Philippines - fast food industry” by Marek Ślusarczyk/Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 3.0)</caption>
    </figure>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00007">
    <title>Culture</title>
    <para id="para-00020">In societies with an individualistic cultural orientation, identity is often based on achieving independence; cultures that are more collectivistic emphasize creating connections within family and communities as important aspects of identity (Muttaqin, 2020). However, this may be affected by globalization. Although Japan is traditionally collectivistic, the current cohort of young adults is more influenced by individualistic values and likely to prioritize independence and finding personally satisfying careers (Arnett et al., 2014; Sugimura, 2020; Sugimura et al., 2023), consistent with the theme of identity exploration and being self-focused. It’s also worth noting that globalization’s influence isn’t identical across contexts. For example, some immigrants assimilate into the culture of their new home country, while others identify more strongly with the culture of their country of origin (Maehler et al., 2019) (<link target-id="fig-00003"/>). Variations in acculturation may thus affect the experience of emerging adulthood for immigrants.</para>
    <figure id="fig-00003">
    <media alt="Alt text placeholder">
    <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/OSX_Lifespan_11_01_CultureInt.jpg"/>
    </media>
    <caption>Globalization provides opportunities for people from different cultures to interact with and influence each other, such as this meeting of young adults at the 2016 Global Entrepreneurship Summit. (credit: modification of work “Global Entrepreneurship Summit 2016” by GES 2016/Flickr, Public Domain)</caption>
    </figure>
    <para id="para-00021">The way a culture regards different subgroups, and the opportunities a culture makes available to its members, may also matter. A review of literature relating to the experiences of Black individuals during emerging adulthood (Hope et al., 2015) found that experiencing discrimination interrupted their exploration of self and career. Different racial and ethnic groups within a geographic location may not have the same access to emerging adulthood if there are discriminatory factors at work. When people have multiple cultural affiliations, identity formation may be influenced by both cultures, though not necessarily equally. For example, a study of Chinese Canadian teenagers, all of whom were children of immigrant parents and 80% of whom were Chinese-born themselves, indicated that female teenagers tended to report more tension between their Canadian and Chinese identities than male teenagers. The researchers attributed these differences to the ways the Chinese and Canadian cultures regarded gender. Canadian culture tends to emphasize gender equality and independence for all, but in Chinese culture, male children are more prized and given more freedom than female children. In other words, male Chinese Canadian teenagers benefited from being part of two cultures that encouraged freedom for them, while their female peers had to try to reconcile the conflicting messages of these cultures (Quan et al., 2022).</para>
    <note class="intersections-contexts" id="note-00005">
    <title>DACA and Emerging Adulthood</title>
    <para id="para-00022">The experience of emerging adulthood can depend on factors such as SES and culture. Another possible influence is immigration status. Being a recent immigrant, particularly with undocumented status, may limit opportunities to explore options and choose a meaningful path in life due to language barriers, financial difficulties, and fear of deportation. However, public policy initiatives may be able to address some of these challenges.</para>
    <para id="para-00023">The Deferred Action for Childhood Arrivals (DACA) relief program was enacted in 2012 to protect undocumented immigrants who were brought to the United States before the age of 16 (and before 2007). President Barack Obama described it as being for “young people who study in our schools, play in our neighborhoods, are friends with our kids, and pledge allegiance to our flag” (Connor, 2023, para. 1; <emphasis effect="italics">Key Facts</emphasis>, 2023). DACA granted a two-year work authorization to prevent young people from being deported (<emphasis effect="italics">Key Facts</emphasis>, 2023). More than 835,000 people were protected during the decade DACA was in effect, and although the program is no longer accepting people, about 580,000 people are still in it (Connor, 2023; <emphasis effect="italics">Key Facts</emphasis>, 2023; Wong et al., 2023)(<link target-id="fig-00004"/>).</para>
    <figure id="fig-00004">
    <media alt="Alt text placeholder">
    <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/OSX_Lifespan_11_01_DACA.jpg"/>
    </media>
    <caption>The DACA program provided many people with opportunities. (credit: “DACA-16” by Susan Ruggles/Flickr, CC BY 2.0)</caption>
    </figure>
    <para id="para-00024">The DACA program is relevant here because of its connection to the developmental tasks of emerging adulthood. Many DACA recipients have participated in emerging adulthood tasks such as attending college, starting careers, and having families, and 7 of 10 indicated that DACA allowed them to get schooling and achieve degrees they would not have been able to attain otherwise (Wong et al., 2023). Additionally, DACA recipients over 25 have a 91% employment rate, with many employed in professions considered “essential”, such as health care and education. Their employment status and income allow them to attain material markers of adulthood such as cars, homes, and health insurance (Svajlenka &amp; Truong, 2021) as well as financially support themselves and their families.</para>
    </note>
    </section>
    </section>
    <section class="summary" id="sect-00008">
    <list id="list-00004">
    <item>Arnett’s theory of emerging adulthood proposes that the time period from ages 18-29 is a time of identity exploration, instability, self-focus, feeling in-between, and being in an age of possibilities. People in this age group are taking on roles and responsibilities of adult life, but gradually, and may not have long-term commitments to careers or relationships.</item>
    <item>Marcia’s theory of identity development proposes there are four statuses to crystalizing one’s identity, depending on a person’s degree of exploration and commitment: identity diffusion (little exploration, no commitment), foreclosure (commitment without exploration), identity moratorium (exploration, but no commitment), and identity achievement (exploration with commitment).</item>
    <item>While emerging adulthood occurs in a variety of contexts and cultures, there may be differences in the way this transition is experienced. Factors such as SES and the degree to which exploration is encouraged or discouraged can influence whether and how individuals explore educational and career options and commit to jobs and relationships</item>
    </list>
    </section>
